description: Minimal comments — only where the code cannot speak for itself
alwaysApply: true
---

# Comments

- Default: **no comment**. Clear names, small functions, and types replace narration.
- **Do** comment when:
  - Platform or product constraint that is not obvious from code (e.g. native runner must match theme color).
  - Non-obvious algorithm, workaround, or `// ignore:` with a one-line why.
  - Public API (`///`) when behavior or preconditions are not obvious from the signature.
- **Do not** comment:
  - What the next line obviously does (`// increment counter`, `// return widget`).
  - Boilerplate section banners or long file headers.
  - Commented-out code — delete it (git keeps history).
  - Large blocks explaining architecture that belongs in `docs/` or `.cursor/rules/`.

```dart
// BAD
// This widget is the root of your application.
@override
Widget build(BuildContext context) { ... }

// GOOD — only when something non-obvious
// macos/Runner/MainFlutterWindow.swift must mirror scaffoldBackgroundColor to avoid a black flash at launch.
```

Same for Kotlin, Swift, YAML, and XML: one short line beats a paragraph.